  message: "DONE for the clear cases (PR #602), with a rule refinement this item did not have.\n\nTHE RULE IS \"IS THE READ STILL REPRESENTED AFTER THE REMOVAL\", not \"does this\ncall issue SQL\". Remove a builder only when the executor is separately rowed.\n\nREMOVED: javax/jakarta EntityManager {createQuery, createNamedQuery,\ncreateNativeQuery} (executor TypedQuery.getResultList is rowed); JDBC\nConnection.createStatement (executor Statement.executeQuery is rowed); all nine\nEcto.Query combinators (executor Ecto.Repo.all is rowed). java's rows are\ninherited by kotlin and scala, so one edit cleared three languages.\n\nDJANGO IS KEPT, REVERSING THIS ITEM'S OWN FILING. I listed django order_by and\ndistinct as defects. They are builders by the same definition -- a QuerySet is\nlazy and filter() issues nothing -- but Django executes through an IMPLICIT\n__iter__ with NO CALL SITE TO CATALOGUE. There is no executor row to relocate\nthe read to, so removing them would DELETE the read rather than move it. Pinned\nas a test that costs something.\n\nKEPT, ruled separately: prepareStatement, prepareCall, Postgrex.prepare,\nMyXQL.prepare -- most drivers send SQL to the server at prepare time.\nEXCLUDED after checking: ets/dets/mnesia.select genuinely execute.\n\nMEASUREMENT IS PARTIAL AND I AM SAYING SO. The elixir arm moved: plausible\ndb_read 55 -> 48 (7 Ecto.Query chains), though no verdict changed. THE JAVA ARM\nIS UNMEASURED -- playframework carries JPA calls but moved no db_read, so the\nEntityManager module slot was never resolved there. The java rows are removed on\nthe argument, not on evidence."  # da31
